From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brian McKenna <brian@brianmckenna.org>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 23:15:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(shader): rebuild T# table loads at runtime indices in
 descriptor webs

An image read's T# can reach the read along several paths: loaded on
each from a table in memory at an index the shader read from a constant
buffer, and holding unrelated words on a path where the read does not
happen (the register's earlier contents, through the structurizer's
merges). The descriptor web rebuilds each path's T# before the read, but
only from registers, the SRT and uniform operations. A load at an index
read from a constant buffer was not rebuilt, so every path was folded
and the read failed with "GetImageResource dword 0 is not a valid
runtime value; ... descriptor web: no path's descriptor can be rebuilt
from supported runtime values". GPU culling compute shaders that test
bounds against a depth pyramid taken from a bindless table stop so.

A load through an address is now rebuilt when its offset is made of
user data, SRT reads, uniform operations and raw scalar reads done
before the image read (those are used as they are, not read again).
Such a path's T# then becomes an image table column keyed by the index,
as a single load at a runtime index already did, and a key read from a
buffer the shader doesn't write still narrows the table to the slots
the dispatch reads. A path whose words mix a table slot with other
words is folded as before. The web's check of the selected T#'s words
is left out when a path reads a table slot: the shader never holds those
words, and the table access checks the slot it reads.

Tested: tests/DescriptorSelectionTests.cpp gains a shader that loads a
T# from a table at one of two indices read at a lane-dependent address,
on two paths, with a third path that holds no T#. It fails without the
change ("GetImageResource dword 0 is not a valid runtime value") and
passes with it under Wine, built against Rebox's MinGW build of the
recompiler, as do the file's other tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
